====================================================================================================
COMPARATIVE SUMMARY: fertility, collisions, and LM perplexity by language
====================================================================================================
Language  bytes/char  %>32B     %colliding  Kron PPL    Fourier PPL   PPL delta
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
english   1.00        0.00      0.00        690.11      694.91        -4.81
hindi     2.53        0.15      0.00        606.18      684.73        -78.54
kannada   2.65        0.70      0.05        840.79      828.16        +12.63
tamil     2.64        1.10      0.40        942.70      984.00        -41.30
telugu    2.57        1.06      0.23        364.38      400.12        -35.74

A positive PPL delta means Fourier achieved LOWER (better) perplexity
than Kronecker on that language's held-out text. If the Fourier codec
is doing what the design predicts, this delta should be largest for
the highest-bytes/char, highest-%-colliding languages, and smallest
(near zero, possibly slightly negative) for English.

====================================================================================================
MODEL SIZE COMPARISON: codec params, share of total model, vs. dense embedding
====================================================================================================
Codec           Codec params    % of total model  Total model params  vs. dense embedding
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
kronecker       1,048,576       26.84             3,906,304           51.2% of dense
fourier         32,768          1.13              2,890,496           1.6% of dense
dense_embedding 2,048,000       41.75             4,905,728           100.0% of dense

At this vocab size (16000) and d_model (128), a dense
nn.Embedding table alone would cost 2,048,000 parameters and ~32.8 MB of AdamW training
state (Session 7 Section 3's 16-bytes-per-parameter accounting: bf16
weight+grad, fp32 master + 2 optimizer moments). Both Kronecker and Fourier
replace that with a MUCH smaller structured codec -- this table shows exactly
how much smaller, and what fraction of the FULL trained model that codec ends
up being (not just its size in isolation, which is what codec_projection_params
reports above).

